el separability matrices.\n\n2. **Specific case**: When we have `Pix2Sky_Gnomonic() & (Linear1D(10) & Linear1D(5))`, the two Linear1D models should remain independent, but they\'re not being handled correctly.\n\n3. **Root cause**: Looking at the `_separable` function (lines 290-312), when dealing with a CompoundModel, it recursively calls `_separable` on both left and right parts and then applies the appropriate operator function.\n\n4. **Issue location**: In the `_cdot` function (lines 250-287), there\'s a line that swaps left and right:\n   ```python\n   left, right = right, left\n   ```\n   \n   This is likely causing problems with the order of operations in nested cases.\n\nLet me examine the code more closely to understand the exact issue.\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=str_replace_editor>\n<parameter=path>\n/testbed/astropy/modeling/separable.py\n</parameter>\n<parameter=command>\nview\n</parameter>\n<parameter=view_range>\n[250, 290]\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n<tool_response>\nOBSERVATION:\n\rHere\'s the result of running `cat -n` on /testbed/astropy/modeling/separable.py:\n   250\tdef _cdot(left, right):\n   251\t    """\n   252\t    Function corresponding to "|" operation.\n   253\t\n   254\t    Parameters\n   255\t    ----------\n   256\t    left, right : `astropy.modeling.Model` or ndarray\n   257\t        If input is of an array, it is the output of `coord_matrix`.\n   258\t\n   259\t    Returns\n   260\t    -------\n   261\t    result : ndarray\n   262\t        Result from this operation.\n   263\t    """\n   264\t\n   265\t    left, right = right, left\n   266\t\n   267\t    def _n_inputs_outputs(input, position):\n   268\t        """\n   269\t        Return ``n_inputs``, ``n_outputs`` for a model or coord_matrix.\n   270\t        """\n   271\t        if isinstance(input, Model):\n   272\t            coords = _coord_matrix(input, position, input.n_outputs)\n   273\t        else:\n   274\t            coords = input\n   275\t        return coords\n   276\t\n   277\t    cleft = _n_inputs_outputs(left, \'left\')\n   278\t    cright = _n_inputs_outputs(right, \'right\')\n   279\t\n   280\t    try:\n   281\t        result = np.dot(cleft, cright)\n   282\t    except ValueError:\n   283\t        raise ModelDefinitionError(\n   284\t            \'Models cannot be combined with the "|" operator; \'\n   285\t            \'left coord_matrix is {}, right coord_matrix is {}\'.format(\n   286\t                cright, cleft))\n   287\t    return result\n   288\t\n   289\t\n   290\tdef _separable(transform):\n\n\r\r\n</tool_response>\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nThe issue is clear now. In the `_cdot` function, line 265 swaps `left` and `right` variables. This is incorrect for the order of operations in compound models. \n\nIn the expression `Pix2Sky_Gnomonic() & (Linear1D(10) & Linear1D(5))`, we have:\n- Left side: `Pix2Sky_Gnomonic()`  \n- Right side: `(Linear1D(10) & Linear1D(5))`\n\nWhen this gets processed, the `&` operation creates a horizontal stack (`_cstack`) and the `|` operation creates a dot product (`_cdot`). But in `_cdot`, the swapping causes the wrong order.\n\nLet me also look at the `_cstack` function to understand the flow:\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=str_replace_editor>\n<parameter=path>\n/testbed/astropy/modeling/separable.py\n</parameter>\n<parameter=command>\nview\n</parameter>\n<parameter=view_range>\n[219, 248]\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n<tool_response>\nOBSERVATION:\n\rHere\'s the result of running `cat -n` on /testbed/astropy/modeling/separable.py:\n   219\tdef _cstack(left, right):\n   220\t    """\n   221\t    Function corresponding to \'&\' operation.\n   222\t\n   223\t    Parameters\n   224\t    ----------\n   225\t    left, right : `astropy.modeling.Model` or ndarray\n   226\t        If input is of an array, it is the output of `coord_matrix`.\n   227\t\n   228\t    Returns\n   229\t    -------\n   230\t    result : ndarray\n   231\t        Result from this operation.\n   232\t\n   233\t    """\n   234\t    noutp = _compute_n_outputs(left, right)\n   235\t\n   236\t    if isinstance(left, Model):\n   237\t        cleft = _coord_matrix(left, \'left\', noutp)\n   238\t    else:\n   239\t        cleft = np.zeros((noutp, left.shape[1]))\n   240\t        cleft[: left.shape[0], : left.shape[1]] = left\n   241\t    if isinstance(right, Model):\n   242\t        cright = _coord_matrix(right, \'right\', noutp)\n   243\t    else:\n   244\t        cright = np.zeros((noutp, right.shape[1]))\n   245\t        cright[-right.shape[0]:, -right.shape[1]:] = 1\n   246\t\n   247\t    return np.hstack([cleft, cright])\n   248\t\n\n\r\r\n</tool_response>\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nNow I understand the complete picture. The issue is in the `_cdot` function where it swaps left and right arguments. This is causing the wrong order in the computation of the separability matrix for nested compound models.\n\n## Phase 4: Fix Implementation\n\nThe fix is straightforward - remove the line that swaps left and right in the `_cdot` function:\n\n<tool_call>\n<function=str_replace_editor>\n<parameter=path>\n/testbed/astropy/modeling/separable.py\n</parameter>\n<parameter=command>\nstr_replace\n</parameter>\n<parameter=old_str>\ndef _cdot(left, right):\n    """\n    Function corresponding to "|" operation.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    left, right : `astropy.modeling.Model` or ndarray\n        If input is of an array, it is the output of `coord_matrix`.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    result : ndarray\n        Result from this operation.\n    """\n\n    left, right = right, left\n</parameter>\n<parameter=new_str>\ndef _cdot(left, right):\n    """\n    Function corresponding to "|" operation.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    left, right : `astropy.modeling.Model` or ndarray\n        If input is of an array, it is the output of `coord_matrix`.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    result : nda